{'name': 'interval_width_power_prior', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': "Refinement of bigelow_piantadosi_independent: people still do Bayesian concept learning over the same 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 with the size principle, but their prior over intervals is not flat — it rises or falls as a power of the interval's width, with the exponent inferred. A", 'context': 'experiment1 round 0 candidate 4 refine incumbent bigelow_piantadosi_independent'}

{'name': 'tempered_size_principle_rules_intervals', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of `tenenbaum_rules_similarity`: people do Bayesian concept learning over mathematical rules and intervals as in that model, but they discount the evidence of the examples — each example counts for only a fraction (or a multiple) of a fully independent random draw from the concept, by a f', 'context': 'experiment1 round 0 candidate 5 refine chosen'}

{'name': 'serial_position_weighted_concept_learning', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': "People read the examples one at a time and integrate them into the same Bayesian concept learning over rules and intervals (with occasional guessing) as the best model, but their memory and attention are unequal across the sequence: each example's evidence is weighted by its serial position, with a ", 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 0 lens 3'}

{'name': 'inferred_example_noise_belief', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'People do Bayesian concept learning over weighted mathematical rules and number intervals with the size principle, but they distort one assumption of the normative learner: how often they believe an example is a random number not produced by the concept. That believed noise rate is a free, inferred ', 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 1 lens 4'}

{'name': 'weak_sampling_concept_elimination', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'People generalize by weak-sampling hypothesis elimination: they entertain the same kinds of candidate concepts (mathematical rules and numeric intervals) but treat the examples only as positive labels on numbers that happened to come up, not as draws from the concept, so every concept consistent wit', 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 2 lens 5'}

{'name': 'example_noise_concept_learning', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of concept_learning_with_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle) with occasional guessing, but how readily they treat an example as a stray "noise" number that does not belong to the concept is a property of', 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 3 refine incumbent concept_learning_with_guessing'}

{'name': 'inferred_noise_concept_learning', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "predicts like existing model 'example_noise_concept_learning' (p_yes RMSE 0.00131 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of example_noise_concept_learning, not a new hypothesis.", 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of concept_learning_with_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle), with the same guessing on a fraction of trials, but the share of examples they believe are random noise rather than drawn from the concept is', 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 4 refine incumbent concept_learning_with_guessing'}

{'name': 'inferred_outlier_rate_concept_learning', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "predicts like existing model 'inferred_example_noise_belief' (p_yes RMSE 0.00166 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of inferred_example_noise_belief, not a new hypothesis.", 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of bigelow_piantadosi_independent: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 with the size principle, but how often they believe an example is a stray, random number rather than a true member of the concept is not fixed at 10% — it ', 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 5 refine chosen'}

{'name': 'tempered_evidence_concept_guessing', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': "Refinement of concept_learning_with_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100, with the same occasional guessing, but they do not weigh the examples as fully independent random draws from the concept — each example's evidence counts for ", 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 4 refine incumbent concept_learning_with_guessing repair 1'}

{'name': 'decision_gain_concept_learning', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of bigelow_piantadosi_independent: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples), but they do not report their belief that the target is in the concept directly — they turn it into a yes/no decision ', 'context': 'experiment1 round 1 candidate 5 refine chosen repair 1'}

{'name': 'win_stay_lose_shift_concept_tracking', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'People do not hold a full posterior over concepts; they hold a single running guess that they revise example by example, in the order shown (win-stay, lose-shift). After the first example they pick a concept in proportion to its Bayesian plausibility (weighted rules and intervals, size principle), k', 'context': 'experiment1 round 2 candidate 0 lens 6'}

{'name': 'digit_grid_exemplar_similarity', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': "People judge a target by its similarity to the examples in the written-digit form of the numbers: each number is a point on a grid of its tens digit and its ones digit, and a target's similarity to an example falls off exponentially with the attention-weighted city-block distance between them on tha", 'context': 'experiment1 round 2 candidate 1 lens 7'}

{'name': 'response_bias_concept_learning', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'People do the same Bayesian concept learning as the simplest rule-and-interval model (42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100, size principle, 10% noisy examples), but they do not report their belief directly: they compare the evidence for "the program makes this number" against a shifted decis', 'context': 'experiment1 round 2 candidate 2 lens 8'}

{'name': 'redundant_examples_tempered_concepts', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of tempered_evidence_concept_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100, with tempered evidence and occasional guessing, but they treat additional examples as partly redundant rather than as independent draws, so the total evid', 'context': 'experiment1 round 2 candidate 3 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_concept_guessing'}

{'name': 'sublinear_evidence_accumulation', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "predicts like existing model 'redundant_examples_tempered_concepts' (p_yes RMSE 0.00130 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of redundant_examples_tempered_concepts, not a new hypothesis.", 'hypothesis': "Refinement of tempered_evidence_concept_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 with tempered evidence and occasional guessing, but the weight each example's evidence carries depends on how many examples they were shown — evidence accu", 'context': 'experiment1 round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_concept_guessing'}

{'name': 'tempered_evidence_decision_gain', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of decision_gain_concept_learning: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples) and turn their belief into a yes/no answer through the same inferred decision gain on the log-odds, but they do not we', 'context': 'experiment1 round 2 candidate 5 refine chosen'}

{'name': 'tempered_evidence_inferred_noise', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "too slow to fit: the fit of 'tempered_evidence_inferred_noise' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood", 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of tempered_evidence_concept_guessing: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100, with tempered evidence (each example counts for an inferred fraction or multiple of a full draw) and occasional guessing, but how often they believe an ex', 'context': 'experiment1 round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_concept_guessing repair 1'}

{'name': 'offset_progression_concepts', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'People do the same Bayesian concept learning as the best model (weighted mathematical rules and all intervals of 1-100, size principle with tempered evidence, and a decision gain turning belief into a yes/no answer), but their space of rules also contains offset arithmetic progressions — "every k-th', 'context': 'experiment1 round 3 candidate 0 lens 9'}

{'name': 'exemplar_averaged_generalization', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'People do not infer one concept from the whole set of examples; instead each example is stored as a separate exemplar, and the target is judged by how strongly each single stored example on its own generalizes to it — through the familiar number categories (mathematical rules and numeric ranges) tha', 'context': 'experiment1 round 3 candidate 1 lens 10'}

{'name': 'predictive_representativeness_judgment', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': "People do not ask whether the target belongs to the concept they have inferred; they ask how representative the target would be as the program's next output — how much more likely the program is to produce exactly this number than a number picked at random, given the concepts the examples suggest (w", 'context': 'experiment1 round 3 candidate 2 lens 0'}

{'name': 'criterion_shifted_tempered_decision', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': "Refinement of tempered_evidence_decision_gain: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples, examples' evidence tempered by an inferred weight) and pass their belief through the same inferred decision gain on i", 'context': 'experiment1 round 3 candidate 3 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_decision_gain'}

{'name': 'tempered_gain_shifted_criterion', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "predicts like existing model 'criterion_shifted_tempered_decision' (p_yes RMSE 0.00000 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of criterion_shifted_tempered_decision, not a new hypothesis.", 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of tempered_evidence_decision_gain: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples) with tempered evidence, and turn their belief into a yes/no answer through the same inferred decision gain on its log', 'context': 'experiment1 round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_decision_gain'}

{'name': 'win_stay_lose_shift_decision_gain', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of win_stay_lose_shift_concept_tracking: people still hold a single running guess about the concept (drawn from the tempered Bayesian posterior over weighted rules and intervals after the first example, kept while later examples fit it, redrawn from the current posterior when an example c', 'context': 'experiment1 round 3 candidate 5 refine chosen'}

{'name': 'redundant_examples_decision_gain', 'outcome': 'admitted', 'detail': '', 'hypothesis': 'Refinement of tempered_evidence_decision_gain: people do the same Bayesian concept learning over the 42 weighted rules and all intervals of 1-100 (size principle, 10% noisy examples, tempered evidence) and turn their belief into a yes/no answer through the same inferred decision gain on its log-odds', 'context': 'experiment1 round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent tempered_evidence_decision_gain repair 1'}

